  {
    "episode_id": 80581,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80581.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, you wonder if it's also sort of a lost opportunity because we'll see what the coverage is like from media there. But it doesn't seem as though there are going to be a lot of questions asked about what's really going on in Xinjiang and what's really going on with Taiwan. And you just wonder if having some of our diplomats there or high-level government officials could have perhaps pressed some of those points in a forum that might have been a little embarrassing and difficult for the Chinese. It's interesting that there was a meeting this week or last week, I guess, with the IOC President, Thomas Bach. He met with Chinese President Xi, and they discussed it. And the IOC put out a statement that said, the two leaders discussed the strong support of the international community for the Olympic winner Games in Beijing 2022. And essentially, they say our responsibility, this is the IOC, is to run the Games in accordance with the Olympic Charter and to bring together the athletes from 206 teams and the IOC refugee team under one roof. So they're saying, look, it's not really our problem, what's going on with the Uighur population, and we're here to run the games.",
