  {
    "episode_id": 80581,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80581.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. And so what I find so hypocritical there is that the IOC rightly, when it came to South Africa and apartheid, took a much different approach. They banned any Olympics from occurring in South Africa, not just one time, but for nearly three decades and took the additional step, of banning any South African athlete in any sport from participating in any Olympic games anywhere else in the world for nearly 30 years over a political issue, over apartheid in the atrocities that were going on to Black South Africans then. So why didn't it matter then? But it doesn't matter with nearly a million Muslims in concentration camps, mass rate, surveillance, torture, and modern day slave labor, not to mention what they've done to the Tibetans, Hong Kong, Christians, and others. And the only difference that I can point to that I think is this logical and reasonable is the amount of money involved. I call out the IOC president on that because they took a very different stand on apartheid, but they don't on Chinese genocide. And again, it's the money involved.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
